<title>USA: Eastern Montana crops still short of moisture.</title>
<headline>Eastern Montana crops still short of moisture.</headline>
<dateline>HELENA, Montana 1997-06-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Many areas in the eastern third of Montana remained short of moisture during the last week, the state's Agricultural Statistics Service reported.</p>
<p>Statewide topsoil moisture was rated 44 percent short or very short, up sharply from 19 percent a week earlier.</p>
<p>Crop progress was ahead of normal for the small grains. Winter wheat had headed on 90 percent of the acreage and had started turning on eight percent. The five year average for the date were 82 percent and eight percent respectively.</p>
<p>Spring wheat had headed on 33 percent, up from an average of 25 percent.</p>
<p>Oats had headed on 32 percent of the acreage, up from a normal 21 percent, while barley was headed on 19 percent, slightly behind the five-year average of 25 percent.</p>
<p>((Chicago newsdesk 312-408-8720))</p>
</text>
